
constructor



Basic Questions
What is a constructor in Java?
A constructor is a special member of a class used to initialize an object. It is automatically called when an object is created.

Why do we use constructors?
To initialize the instance variables of an object when the object is created.

What are the rules for a constructor?

Constructor name must be the same as the class name.

It cannot have a return type.

It is called automatically when an object is created.

It can have parameters.

It cannot be static, abstract, or final.

Does a constructor have a return type?
No. A constructor does not have any return type, not even void.

When is a constructor called?
When an object is created using the new keyword.

Student s = new Student();

Can a constructor be private?
Yes. A private constructor prevents other classes from directly creating objects of that class.

Can a constructor be overloaded?
Yes. A class can have multiple constructors with different parameter lists.

class Student {
    Student() { }

    Student(String name) { }

    Student(String name, int age) { }
}

Can a constructor be overridden?
No. Constructors are not inherited, so they cannot be overridden.

Can a constructor be inherited?
No. Constructors belong to their own class and are not inherited by subclasses.

Can a constructor be static?
No. A constructor is associated with object creation, while static members belong to the class.

Types of Constructors
What are the types of constructors in Java?

Commonly discussed types are:

Default constructor

No-argument constructor

Parameterized constructor

Copy constructor

What is a no-argument constructor?

A constructor that does not accept any parameters.

class Student {
    Student() {
        System.out.println("Constructor called");
    }
}

What is a parameterized constructor?

A constructor that accepts one or more parameters.

class Student {
    String name;

    Student(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

What is a default constructor?

If you don't declare any constructor, the Java compiler automatically provides a constructor with no parameters. This is commonly called the default constructor.

What is a copy constructor?

A constructor that creates a new object by copying values from another object of the same class.

Student(Student s) {
    this.name = s.name;
}

Java does not provide a special built-in copy-constructor feature; we define such a constructor ourselves.

Important Interview Questions
What happens if we don't create a constructor?

If the class has no constructor declared, the compiler provides a default no-argument constructor.

What happens if we create a parameterized constructor but no no-argument constructor?

The compiler will not provide a no-argument constructor automatically.

class Student {
    Student(String name) {
    }
}

Student s = new Student(); // ❌ Compilation error

Can a constructor call another constructor?

Yes. We use this().

class Student {
    Student() {
        this("Rahul");
    }

    Student(String name) {
        System.out.println(name);
    }
}

What is this() in a constructor?

this() is used to call another constructor of the same class.

What is super() in a constructor?

super() is used to call the constructor of the parent class.

class Animal {
    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {
        super();
        System.out.println("Dog");
    }
}

Can we use both this() and super() in the same constructor?

No. Both must be the first statement of a constructor, so they cannot both be used in the same constructor.

Where must this() or super() appear?

It must be the first statement inside the constructor.

What happens if we don't write super() in a child constructor?

Java automatically inserts a call to the parent's no-argument constructor, if one is accessible.

Can a constructor call a method?

Yes.

class Student {
    Student() {
        display();
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}

Can a constructor be overloaded?

Yes.

Student() { }

Student(String name) { }

Student(String name, int age) { }

Can a constructor be final?

No. Constructors cannot be final.

Can a constructor be abstract?

No. Constructors cannot be abstract.

Can a constructor be synchronized?

No. Constructors cannot be declared synchronized.

Can a constructor be native?

No. Constructors cannot be declared native.

Can we use return inside a constructor?

You cannot return a value from a constructor. A bare return; can be used to exit the constructor early.

Tricky Interview Questions
Is a constructor a method?

Technically, no. Although it looks similar to a method, a constructor has special rules and is specifically used for object initialization.

Can we create an object without calling a constructor?

In normal object creation with new, a constructor is invoked. However, Java provides special mechanisms such as deserialization and reflection that can create objects under different rules.

Can a constructor be overloaded with different parameter types?

Yes.

Student(int age) { }

Student(String name) { }

Can a constructor have the same name as another constructor?

Yes, provided their parameter lists are different. This is constructor overloading.

Can a constructor be called directly like a method?

No.

Student(); // ❌

Constructors are invoked during object creation or through constructor chaining such as this() and super().

What is constructor chaining?

When one constructor calls another constructor, it is called constructor chaining.

It can happen:

Within the same class using this()

Between parent and child classes using super()

Which executes first: parent constructor or child constructor?

The parent constructor executes first, followed by the child constructor.

Parent Constructor
       ↓
Child Constructor

Can an abstract class have a constructor?

Yes. An abstract class can have a constructor, and it is called when a subclass object is created.

Can an interface have a constructor?

No. Interfaces do not have constructors because they cannot be instantiated.

Can an enum have a constructor?

Yes. Enum constructors exist, but they cannot be called directly by using new.

Very Common Output Question
Question: What is the output?

class A {
    A() {
        System.out.println("A");
    }
}

class B extends A {
    B() {
        System.out.println("B");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        B obj = new B();
    }
}

Answer:

A
B

Because the parent constructor executes before the child constructor.

